APS-002 — Policy Principles

Document ID: APS-002
Title: Policy Principles
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification establishes the fundamental principles governing the design, implementation, evaluation, enforcement, and evolution of policies within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

These principles constitute the normative foundation upon which all Atlas policies SHALL be defined and interpreted.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Foundational policy principles
Policy behavior
Policy integrity
Policy governance
Policy quality
Policy evolution
Policy interoperability

These principles apply to every Canonical Policy Object (CPO).

3. Canonical Definition

A Policy Principle is a normative statement defining an immutable characteristic that every Atlas policy SHALL satisfy regardless of implementation, technology, or engineering domain.

Policy principles constrain policy design.

They do not define individual policies.

4. Objectives

Policy Principles SHALL:

Preserve engineering integrity.
Ensure consistent governance.
Enable trustworthy automation.
Support explainability.
Promote interoperability.
Enable deterministic behavior.
Support evolution.
Preserve long-term stability.
5. Foundational Principles

Atlas defines the following foundational principles.

PP-001 — Explicitness

Policies SHALL be explicitly defined.

No implicit behavior SHALL be assumed.

PP-002 — Determinism

Equivalent inputs SHALL produce equivalent policy decisions unless the policy explicitly declares adaptive behavior.

PP-003 — Explainability

Every policy decision SHALL provide sufficient information to explain:

Applicable policy
Evaluation path
Conditions satisfied
Conditions failed
Final outcome
PP-004 — Traceability

Every policy evaluation SHALL preserve:

Inputs
Evaluation steps
Decision
Responsible policy version
Supporting evidence

Traceability SHALL remain immutable.

PP-005 — Technology Independence

Policy semantics SHALL remain independent of:

Programming languages
Rule engines
Databases
Cloud providers
Infrastructure
Transport protocols
PP-006 — Separation of Policy and Execution

Policies define permissible behavior.

Execution mechanisms implement that behavior.

Policies SHALL NOT embed implementation logic.

PP-007 — Governance

Every policy SHALL be:

Owned
Versioned
Reviewed
Approved
Auditable

Policies SHALL themselves be governed objects.

PP-008 — Least Authority

Policies SHALL grant only the minimum authority necessary to accomplish the intended objective.

PP-009 — Composability

Policies SHALL be composable without altering their individual semantic meaning.

Composition SHALL preserve policy independence.

PP-010 — Conflict Awareness

Policies SHALL support explicit conflict detection.

Conflicting policies SHALL NOT silently override one another.

PP-011 — Version Awareness

Every policy evaluation SHALL reference the exact policy version used.

Historical evaluations SHALL remain reproducible.

PP-012 — Context Sensitivity

Policies MAY depend upon context.

Applicable context SHALL be explicitly defined.

Context SHALL NOT be inferred without authorization.

PP-013 — Reusability

Policies SHOULD be reusable across:

Projects
Organizations
Domains
Engineering disciplines

Policy duplication SHOULD be minimized.

PP-014 — Layered Governance

Higher-level policies SHALL govern lower-level policies.

Lower-level policies SHALL NOT violate higher-level policies.

PP-015 — Continuous Evolution

Policies MAY evolve.

Evolution SHALL preserve:

History
Version lineage
Traceability
Governance
6. Policy Quality Principles

Every policy SHALL satisfy:

Correctness
Completeness
Consistency
Determinism
Explainability
Traceability
Testability
Maintainability

Quality SHALL be continuously evaluated.

7. Policy Lifecycle Principles

Every policy SHALL support:

Draft
Review
Approval
Publication
Active Use
Deprecation
Retirement

Lifecycle transitions SHALL remain governed.

8. Policy Interoperability Principles

Policies SHALL support:

Cross-platform interoperability
Cross-organization exchange
Semantic preservation
Version compatibility
Federation

Interoperability SHALL preserve policy meaning.

9. Policy Security Principles

Policies SHALL protect:

Confidentiality
Integrity
Availability
Authenticity
Non-repudiation

Security requirements SHALL themselves be governed by policy.

10. Policy Governance Principles

Every policy SHALL define:

Owner
Steward
Approval authority
Review process
Audit requirements
Retention policy

Governance SHALL remain attached throughout the policy lifecycle.

11. Conformance

A policy conforms to this specification if it:

Satisfies all foundational principles.
Supports governance.
Supports explainability.
Supports traceability.
Supports versioning.
Supports lifecycle management.
Supports interoperability.
12. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Domain-specific principle profiles.
AI-assisted policy validation.
Adaptive policy principles.
International governance profiles.
Formal policy verification.
Autonomous policy governance.

Extensions SHALL preserve the canonical policy principles.

